riscos-linux-testbed: QEMU 8.2.2 linux-user changes the Linux port of RISC OS
needs, ported from Timothy Baldwin's qemu_swi.diff for QEMU 5.2
(RISC_OS_Linux_Binary, Unix/LinuxSupport/qemu_swi.diff):

1. linux-user/arm/cpu_loop.c: SWI intercept. The port sets a range with
   SWI &C0001 (r0 = base, r1 = limit, r2 = FPA emulation on/off); a SWI
   from outside that range is a RISC OS SWI and raises SIGSYS, as a seccomp
   SECCOMP_RET_TRAP filter does on a real ARM Linux.
2. linux-user/signal.c: real-time signals keep their numbers (the port
   passes 40 and 41 to fcntl F_SETSIG, which QEMU doesn't translate).
3. linux-user/elfload.c: don't reserve the whole span of an ET_EXEC; the
   port's sparse ROM image needs the gaps for later allocations.
   (Ours, also elfload.c and main.c:) where the host's mmap_min_addr is
   above one page, use a guest_base instead of mapping the guest at its
   own addresses: the port maps from 0x1000, which a host with
   mmap_min_addr 65536 refuses unless the process has CAP_SYS_RAWIO (so it
   failed in containers, e.g. GitHub Actions jobs). RISCOS_IDENTITY_MAP
   turns that off.
4. (ours, not in qemu_swi.diff) target/arm/tcg/translate.c and
   linux-user/arm/cpu_loop.c: privileged PSR instructions - MRS CPSR, MSR
   CPSR with the control field, CPS - in code outside the port's own
   (0x20000000-0x20C00000: modules and programs loaded from disc) are
   emulated with the port's virtual CPU modes, through its PSR HAL
   routines, as they work on a real machine. On Linux they'd read user
   mode and ignore the change: HardDisc4 5.30's SharedCLibrary 6.22 enters
   USR mode with MSR, then hangs. Also emulated there, as the port's
   PVirt routines do them: MRS/MSR SPSR, STM/LDM with ^ (USR's r13, r14)
   and exception returns (LDM {..., pc}^, MOVS/SUBS/ADDS pc, ...): these
   are UNDEF in Linux user mode, and CLib 6.22 uses them to resume a
   program after a handler. In USR mode, MSR and CPS leave the mode and
   interrupt mask alone (MSR sets only the flags), as on a real CPU: BASIC
   VFP drops to USR mode with CPSIE when it's already there. Set
   RISCOS_NO_PSR_EMULATION to turn it off; RISCOS_PSR_DEBUG reports the
   undefined instructions that aren't emulated (FPA ones are normal: the
   FPEmulator handles them). Also MRC/MCR p15 (CP15): reads of the
   control register (SCTLR), main ID and cache type registers give
   plausible values and writes are ignored: Iris's code reads SCTLR.
   The same emulation covers some of the ROM's own modules: the sound
   modules (SoundDMA, SoundChannels, SoundScheduler, StringLib,
   Percussion), never converted to the port's PVirt mode changes (it had
   no sound) and still changing mode with plain MSRs, which Linux ignores:
   SoundScheduler's events crashed. They're found by title in the ROM's
   module chain; RISCOS_PSR_ROM_MODULES gives another list ("" for none).
5. (ours) linux-user/arm/cpu_loop.c: PMP dynamic areas for the test bed's
   ARMEABISupport (lite) page allocators (SOManager's shared library
   data). SWI &C0002 from outside the ROM backs a dynamic area's address
   range with a memfd ("physical" page n = file page n) and maps, unmaps
   and frees pages; OS_DynamicArea 22 on such an area is done here, not
   by RISC OS. Data aborts in one go first to the abort handlers
   registered with SWI &C0002 (SOManager's, which map a task's copy of a
   library's data in), which return through SWI &C0003.
6. (ours) linux-user/arm/cpu_loop.c: what GCCSDK's ARMEABISupport 1.08
   (as Iris ships) needs from the kernel. The port creates physical
   memory pool areas (OS_DynamicArea 0, flag bit 20) but its
   DynArea_PMP_PhysOp/LogOp/Resize/GetPages are stubs that stop on an
   undefined instruction: breakpoints on them (found by name in the ROM)
   do them here, on a memfd per pool, and keep the kernel node's sizes
   current. OS_Memory 0 for application space gives a made-up physical
   address from the file the port maps there for the task (1.08 tells
   programs apart by it); for other addresses (RISC OS's own workspace,
   such as SoundDMA's buffers, which it looks up when it starts) it gives
   the logical address, where the port would refuse (only DMA would use
   it, and there is none), and MRC p15 c6/c5 read the last data abort's
   address and status. RISCOS_SWI_TRACE lists the SWIs RISC OS code
   calls, RISCOS_PMP_DEBUG the pool operations, RISCOS_NO_PMP turns the
   stubs' breakpoints off. linux-user/mmap.c counts guest mappings so
   that the OS_Memory 0 answer is only worked out again after one. A
   return from an exception to Thumb code (an abort taken in it) resumes
   in Thumb state. SWIs called through OS_CallASWI(R12) are looked at
   too. RISCOS_REPORT prints Report_Text0 messages (no !Reporter).
   RISCOS_SWI_RESULT=<hex SWI> reports how that SWI returns (V, r0).
7. (ours) linux-user/syscall.c: getdents and getdents64 leave out "."
   and ".." for the port. Its IXFS numbers a directory's entries in
   getdents order, counting those two though it hides them, so OS_GBPB
   offsets had gaps; UnixLib's readdir needs them to go up by one per
   entry and stopped (empty listings past about 25 entries: GTK's icon
   themes, fontconfig's fonts).
8. (ours) linux-user/signal.c, arm/signal.c, arm/cpu_loop.c: a fault
   signal delivered to the port (from a host SIGBUS or SIGSEGV too, not
   only a data abort QEMU raises itself) sets what MRC p15 c6 (DFAR)
   reads, and so does a signal frame that can't be written to the guest
   stack. ARMEABISupport 1.08's abort handler maps pages at DFAR: with a
   stale one it mapped the same page for ever (TubeSearch, with AcornSSL,
   stopped the desktop). IOC timer 0 (&3200040/44/4C) is emulated:
   AcornSSL reads it for entropy and the port has no IOC.
   RISCOS_SIG_DEBUG lists the signals delivered; RISCOS_PMP_DEBUG=2 each
   abort and pool page operation.

9. (ours) Speed (measured with ROBench): the ROM's DynArea_PMP stubs are
   trapped when translated (riscos_stub_pc, EXCP_RISCOS_STUB) instead of
   breakpoints, which slowed every jump between translated blocks; an
   SWI outside [sys_base, sys_limit] is done in the translated code
   (HELPER(riscos_swi) -> riscos_swi_inline: riscos_host_swi, then
   riscos_fast_swi, the port's ENTER_EXCEPTION(SVC) without a host
   signal; RISCOS_NO_FASTSWI, RISCOS_NO_INLINE_SWI turn them off);
   __kernel_get_tls and __kernel_memory_barrier are generated inline
   (the port finds its vcpu with get_tls several times per SWI); MRS
   Rd, CPSR outside the port's own code is generated inline (flags plus
   the vcpu's mode byte) instead of trapped; MSR writing only flags
   doesn't rebuild hflags. RISCOS_EXCP_STATS counts exits to cpu_loop.

10. (ours) Floating point and NEON (measured with ROBench and checked
   bit for bit by tests/programs/t_fpconform and t_neonint):
   - fpu/softfloat.c: a host FPU fast path for add, subtract, multiply,
     divide and square root (single and double), tried before QEMU's
     hardfloat. It runs the operation on the host and keeps the result
     when it is a finite normal number above the smallest normal (or an
     exact zero from a zero operand or from x - x); then the only flag
     the guest can see is inexact, worked out exactly when it isn't set
     yet (TwoSum; exact double products for single precision; FMA or
     Dekker's product for double). QEMU's hardfloat needs the inexact
     flag already set, so exact arithmetic (integers held in floats)
     always took the slow soft path. Everything else - other rounding
     modes, NaNs, infinities, denormals, flushed inputs - goes to the
     soft code. hostfp_vec_f32() does NEON's four float lanes at once
     (VADD, VSUB, VMUL, VMLA, VMLS, also by scalar) with host vectors;
     target/arm/tcg/vec_helper.c uses it.
   - target/arm/tcg/translate-vfp.c: VMRS APSR_nzcv, FPSCR (FMSTAT, after
     every VCMP) writes QEMU's flag variables directly instead of calling
     helper_cpsr_write.
   - target/arm/tcg/translate-neon.c, neon_helper.c, helper.h: one
     helper per instruction (written as lane loops the host compiler
     vectorises) for the 8- and 16-bit long ops (VADDL, VSUBL, VADDW,
     VSUBW, VABDL, VABAL, VMULL, VMLAL, VMLSL), the narrowing shifts
     (VSHRN, VRSHRN, VQSHRUN, VQRSHRUN, VQSHRN, VQRSHRN), VMOVL/VSHLL and
     VMUL/VMLA/VMLS .I16 by scalar, where QEMU made two to six helper
     calls of 32 or 64 bits each.
   RISCOS_NO_HOSTFP and RISCOS_NO_HOSTNEON turn them off (to compare).

11. (ours) Floating point inline in the translated code (target/arm/tcg/
   riscos-fp.h; checked bit for bit by tests/programs/t_fpconform, one
   instruction at a time, and t_fpchain and t_fpchain_long, generated
   blocks of them in ARM and Thumb, with it on and off, on x86 and
   aarch64 hosts):
   - include/tcg/tcg-opc.h, tcg/tcg.c, tcg/tcg-op-vec.c, the i386 (AVX) and
     aarch64 back ends: vector opcodes for IEEE add, subtract, multiply,
     divide and square root on single or double lanes (rofadd_vec ...),
     signed int/float conversions (rofitof_vec, rofftoi_vec), a 32-bit
     store from a vector register (rofst32_vec; tcg/optimize.c forgets
     what it knew about the memory it writes) and the low halves of two
     vectors joined (rofcat_vec: a NEON Q register is loaded as its two D
     halves, because VLD1 stores it as two 64-bit writes, which a 128-bit
     load can't take from the store buffer).
   - VADD, VSUB, VMUL, VNMUL, VDIV, VSQRT, VMLA, VMLS, VNMLA, VNMLS (VFP,
     single and double) and NEON's VADD, VSUB, VMUL, VMLA, VMLS (also by
     scalar) use them. The host gives the guest's result whenever it is a
     finite normal number above the smallest normal (or an exact zero:
     x - x, 0 * x), the rounding is to nearest and no input would be
     flushed (no denormal input when FZ is set: NEON always, VFP in
     "RunFast"), and then the only flag is inexact: the fast path runs only
     when the guest's inexact flag is already set (vfp_helper.c: an FPSCR
     write now sets it in the NEON status too). Each result lane is checked
     with integer vector instructions; if one fails, or the gate does, the
     helper (the old code) runs instead from the untouched inputs.
   - The TB lookup key has the VFP FZ bit and "not rounding to nearest"
     (cpu.h TBFLAG_A32 ROFP_FZ, ROFP_NOTRN, set in helper.c's
     cpu_get_tb_cpu_state): a TB is translated for one setting (FZ: input
     checks; not to nearest: helpers, as before). And for a TB translated
     before the program's first inexact result (or code that never has
     one), a failing gate's slow path goes inline, with a join, instead of
     leaving the TB every time.
   - Done with integer instructions: VCMP/VCMPE (no NaN or denormal
     operand), VABS and VNEG (the sign bit), VCVT integer to double (always
     exact), to single (exact ones), single to double, double to single
     (normal results), float to integer toward zero (in range); NEON's
     VCEQ/VCGE/VCGT (also with #0), VACGE/VACGT, VMAX/VMIN, VABD and VCVT
     between float and integer.
   - The slow paths (the helper calls) are emitted at the end of the TB
     (translate.c: rofp_emit_slow), and continue at the next instruction
     through a TB lookup; the fast paths run straight on, so TCG's
     optimizer keeps loaded and stored values from one to the next, and
     the gate (the status check) is generated once per TB. Inline with a
     join where that can't be done (IT blocks, single-step). The fast
     paths' temps are freed after each instruction (else a few dozen of
     them would use up TCG_MAX_TEMPS, and QEMU would translate the TB
     again in smaller pieces). The list of them is emptied at the start
     of each translation: when TCG does run out (a long TB with its slow
     paths inline) it abandons the translation and starts again.
     At most ROFP_TB_MAX (16) of them in a TB (arm_post_translate_insn):
     a slow path taken goes on in a new TB at the next instruction, and
     long straight-line code would be translated again from each.
   - tcg/optimize.c: fold_sub_to_neg no longer turns 0 - constant into a
     neg_vec of a constant (fold_neg can't fold vectors and aborted; hosts
     with neg_vec, such as aarch64).
   RISCOS_NO_INLINEFP turns it off (to compare).

12. (ours) NEON integer inline in the translated code
   (target/arm/tcg/riscos-neon.h), where QEMU calls a helper per lane:
   the long and wide forms (VADDL/W, VSUBL/W, VMULL, VMLAL, VMLSL, VABDL,
   VABAL), the narrowing shifts (VSHRN, VRSHRN, VQSHRN, VQRSHRN, VQSHRUN,
   VQRSHRUN; QC set from a widen-and-compare), VSHLL, VMOVL, 16-bit
   multiply by scalar, VZIP and VUZP, and VLD2/VLD4 and VST2/VST4 of 8 and
   16 bits (loaded or stored as 64-bit accesses, then unzipped or zipped
   in registers). New TCG vector ops for them (tcg-opc.h: rouxtl, rosxtl,
   rosqxtn, rosqxtun, rozip1, rozip2, ropack; TCG_TARGET_HAS_roint_vec)
   on x86-64 (PMOVZX/SX, PACKSS/US, PUNPCKL/H) and AArch64 (UXTL/SXTL,
   SQXTN/SQXTUN, ZIP1/ZIP2, UZP1); other hosts use the helpers.
   Rounding narrows add the rounding bit after the shift, so no lane can
   overflow. Checked with tests/programs/t_neonint (324 instructions)
   and t_neonperm (236 permute and structure load/store forms): the same
   CRCs with and without, on x86-64 and AArch64. ROBench neon, x86-64:
   blend 3.4x, yuv2rgb 5.6x, sad 2.1x; AArch64 2.3x, 4.5x, 1.6x.
   RISCOS_NO_INLINENEON turns it off (to compare).

13. (ours) Cheaper TB lookups (an indirect branch or a return looks up
   its next TB; that was 35% of FFmpeg's H.264 decoding):
   - target/arm/tcg/hflags.c: A32's VECLEN, VECSTRIDE, VFPEN and ROFP_FZ/
     ROFP_NOTRN are cached in hflags instead of computed at each lookup
     (cpu_get_tb_cpu_state; XScale keeps the old way). vfp_set_fpscr and
     FPEXC writes (VMSR, signal return, gdbstub) rebuild hflags. Checked
     with --enable-debug-tcg, whose assert_hflags_rebuild_correctly
     compares them at every lookup (whole test suite).
   - accel/tcg/translator.c: in user mode, direct jumps are chained across
     pages too (the same-page rule is for system mode's changing
     mappings; here page_set_flags or a write invalidates a page's TBs,
     which unlinks the jumps into them). RISCOS_NO_XPAGE_CHAIN turns it
     off (to compare).
   FFmpeg H.264 720p decode 7.19 s -> 5.65 s (21%); ROBench SWI 1.26x,
   LZ77 compress 1.37x (1.5x on an AArch64 host), the rest about the same.

Applied on top of riscos-crossdev's qemu-8.2.2-align-trap.patch.
QEMU is GPL-2.0-or-later; so are these changes.

diff --git a/accel/tcg/translator.c b/accel/tcg/translator.c
index 38c3400..8bb32c2 100644
--- a/accel/tcg/translator.c
+++ b/accel/tcg/translator.c
@@ -119,6 +119,26 @@ bool translator_use_goto_tb(DisasContextBase *db, vaddr dest)
         return false;
     }
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_USER_ONLY
+    /*
+     * RISC OS testbed: in user mode, chain to any page. The page check is
+     * there for system mode, where a virtual page's mapping can change
+     * under a chained jump; here a page's code is only invalidated by
+     * page_set_flags (munmap, mprotect without exec, a new mapping) or a
+     * write to it, and invalidating a TB unlinks the jumps into it
+     * (tb_jmp_unlink). The flags are checked when a jump is linked, as
+     * for jumps within the page. RISCOS_NO_XPAGE_CHAIN turns it off.
+     */
+    {
+        static int xpage = -1;
+        if (xpage < 0) {
+            xpage = getenv("RISCOS_NO_XPAGE_CHAIN") == NULL;
+        }
+        if (xpage) {
+            return true;
+        }
+    }
+#endif
     /* Check for the dest on the same page as the start of the TB.  */
     return ((db->pc_first ^ dest) & TARGET_PAGE_MASK) == 0;
 }
diff --git a/fpu/softfloat.c b/fpu/softfloat.c
index 027a8e5..81ba288 100644
--- a/fpu/softfloat.c
+++ b/fpu/softfloat.c
@@ -232,6 +232,7 @@ GEN_INPUT_FLUSH3(float64_input_flush3, float64)
 # define QEMU_SOFTFLOAT_ATTR QEMU_FLATTEN __attribute__((noinline))
 #endif
 
+
 static inline bool can_use_fpu(const float_status *s)
 {
     if (QEMU_NO_HARDFLOAT) {
@@ -263,6 +264,388 @@ typedef union {
     double h;
 } union_float64;
 
+/*
+ * RISC OS testbed: a host FPU fast path for add, subtract, multiply, divide
+ * and square root, used before QEMU's own hardfloat code.
+ *
+ * QEMU's hardfloat only runs when the guest's inexact flag is already set
+ * (it can't tell whether an operation was exact) and checks that both
+ * inputs are normal or zero first. This one runs the operation on the host,
+ * then looks only at the result: if it is a finite normal number greater
+ * than the smallest normal, the guest would get the same bits, and the only
+ * flag the operation can raise is inexact. That is worked out exactly when
+ * the flag isn't set yet (an error-free transformation: TwoSum for add and
+ * subtract; for multiply, divide and square root, whether the product
+ * r*b == a holds exactly, in double for single precision, with FMA or
+ * Dekker's product for double). Anything else - zero, denormal, infinite or
+ * NaN results, other rounding modes, denormal inputs when they would be
+ * flushed - goes to the soft code, which gets every case right.
+ * RISCOS_NO_HOSTFP=1 turns it off (to compare against).
+ */
+static bool hostfp_off;
+
+static void __attribute__((constructor)) hostfp_init(void)
+{
+    hostfp_off = getenv("RISCOS_NO_HOSTFP") != NULL;
+}
+
+static inline bool hostfp_ok(const float_status *s)
+{
+    return likely(!hostfp_off &&
+                  s->float_rounding_mode == float_round_nearest_even);
+}
+
+/* an input the soft code would flush to zero (and flag) */
+static inline bool hostfp_f32_flushin(uint32_t a, const float_status *s)
+{
+    return unlikely(s->flush_inputs_to_zero) &&
+           (uint32_t)((a << 1) - 1) < 0x00fffffeu;
+}
+
+static inline bool hostfp_f64_flushin(uint64_t a, const float_status *s)
+{
+    return unlikely(s->flush_inputs_to_zero) &&
+           (uint64_t)((a << 1) - 1) < 0x001ffffffffffffeull;
+}
+
+static inline bool hostfp_f32_res(uint32_t r)
+{
+    uint32_t m = r & 0x7fffffff;
+    return likely(m > 0x00800000 && m < 0x7f800000);
+}
+
+static inline bool hostfp_f64_res(uint64_t r)
+{
+    uint64_t m = r & 0x7fffffffffffffffull;
+    return likely(m > 0x0010000000000000ull && m < 0x7ff0000000000000ull);
+}
+
+static inline bool hostfp_exact(const float_status *s)
+{
+    return !(s->float_exception_flags & float_flag_inexact);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Whether a * b == c exactly, for doubles; -1 when it can't be told cheaply
+ * (operands near the ends of the range), so the caller uses the soft code.
+ */
+static int __attribute__((optimize("fp-contract=off")))
+hostfp_f64_prod_is(double a, double b, double c)
+{
+    union_float64 ua = { .h = a }, ub = { .h = b }, uc = { .h = c };
+    unsigned ea = (ua.s >> 52) & 0x7ff, eb = (ub.s >> 52) & 0x7ff;
+    unsigned ec = (uc.s >> 52) & 0x7ff;
+    /* the residual must not underflow, the split must not overflow */
+    if (ec < 200 || ec > 2000 || ea < 60 || eb < 60 || ea > 2000 || eb > 2000) {
+        return -1;
+    }
+#ifdef __FP_FAST_FMA
+    return fma(a, b, -c) == 0;
+#else
+    double p = a * b;
+    if (p != c) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+    double t = 134217729.0 * a, ah = t - (t - a), al = a - ah;
+    t = 134217729.0 * b;
+    double bh = t - (t - b), bl = b - bh;
+    return (((ah * bh - p) + ah * bl + al * bh) + al * bl) == 0;
+#endif
+}
+
+static inline bool hostfp_f32_addsub(float32 a, float32 b, float_status *s,
+                                     bool sub, float32 *res)
+{
+    union_float32 ua = { .s = a }, ub = { .s = b }, ur;
+    if (!hostfp_ok(s) || hostfp_f32_flushin(a, s) ||
+        hostfp_f32_flushin(b, s)) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    if (sub) {
+        ub.h = -ub.h;
+    }
+    ur.h = ua.h + ub.h;
+    if (!hostfp_f32_res(ur.s)) {
+        /* a zero sum is exact (x + -x, or zeros): IEEE signs, no flags */
+        if ((ur.s << 1) == 0 && ((ua.s << 1) < 0xff000000u) && ((ub.s << 1) < 0xff000000u)) {
+            *res = ur.s;
+            return true;
+        }
+        return false;
+    }
+    if (hostfp_exact(s)) {
+        float bb = ur.h - ua.h;
+        if ((ua.h - (ur.h - bb)) + (ub.h - bb) != 0) {
+            s->float_exception_flags |= float_flag_inexact;
+        }
+    }
+    *res = ur.s;
+    return true;
+}
+
+static inline bool hostfp_f64_addsub(float64 a, float64 b, float_status *s,
+                                     bool sub, float64 *res)
+{
+    union_float64 ua = { .s = a }, ub = { .s = b }, ur;
+    if (!hostfp_ok(s) || hostfp_f64_flushin(a, s) ||
+        hostfp_f64_flushin(b, s)) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    if (sub) {
+        ub.h = -ub.h;
+    }
+    ur.h = ua.h + ub.h;
+    if (!hostfp_f64_res(ur.s)) {
+        if ((ur.s << 1) == 0 && ((ua.s << 1) < 0xffe0000000000000ull) &&
+            ((ub.s << 1) < 0xffe0000000000000ull)) {
+            *res = ur.s;
+            return true;
+        }
+        return false;
+    }
+    if (hostfp_exact(s)) {
+        double bb = ur.h - ua.h;
+        if ((ua.h - (ur.h - bb)) + (ub.h - bb) != 0) {
+            s->float_exception_flags |= float_flag_inexact;
+        }
+    }
+    *res = ur.s;
+    return true;
+}
+
+/* op: 0 multiply, 1 divide */
+static inline bool hostfp_f32_muldiv(float32 a, float32 b, float_status *s,
+                                     int op, float32 *res)
+{
+    union_float32 ua = { .s = a }, ub = { .s = b }, ur;
+    if (!hostfp_ok(s) || hostfp_f32_flushin(a, s) ||
+        hostfp_f32_flushin(b, s)) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    ur.h = op ? ua.h / ub.h : ua.h * ub.h;
+    if (!hostfp_f32_res(ur.s)) {
+        /*
+         * a zero result from a zero operand (0 * x, 0 / x, x finite) is
+         * exact; one from a tiny result underflowed: the soft code
+         */
+        if ((ur.s << 1) == 0 &&
+            ((ua.s << 1) == 0 || (!op && (ub.s << 1) == 0))) {
+            *res = ur.s;
+            return true;
+        }
+        return false;
+    }
+    if (hostfp_exact(s)) {
+        /* products of two singles are exact in double */
+        bool exact = op ? (double)ur.h * (double)ub.h == (double)ua.h
+                        : (double)ua.h * (double)ub.h == (double)ur.h;
+        if (!exact) {
+            s->float_exception_flags |= float_flag_inexact;
+        }
+    }
+    *res = ur.s;
+    return true;
+}
+
+static inline bool hostfp_f64_muldiv(float64 a, float64 b, float_status *s,
+                                     int op, float64 *res)
+{
+    union_float64 ua = { .s = a }, ub = { .s = b }, ur;
+    if (!hostfp_ok(s) || hostfp_f64_flushin(a, s) ||
+        hostfp_f64_flushin(b, s)) {
+        return false;
+    }
